seg-6080a05ace19 = Does the <em>combined</em> PNT solution hold?

# text
seg-3f74a708a746 = The hybrid pack is the punchline: a navigation solution needs <em>both</em> good time <em>and</em> good position. It shows that an optical timing link can keep even a modest clock locked — which means the <b>inertial</b> sensor becomes the weakest link, and that is exactly where a quantum accelerometer pays off.

# text
seg-5f55bb0fd7c5 = Beyond the four packs

# text
# c1: kshana kinds
seg-60191a2cd150 = The same engine runs many more scenario <em>kinds</em>; <c1/> lists them all. They reach past the sensors to the signals and the geometry the sensors depend on:

# text
seg-995ed414d83d = <b>Where the satellites are</b> — orbit propagation, constellation design around the Earth, the Moon or another body, and the positions of the solar-system bodies.

# text
seg-bc8abce2a748 = <b>What the receiver can see</b> — an azimuth-dependent skyline (a street, a quay, a fjord) over the elevation mask, and the sky geometry it leaves: the satellites in view, their dilution of precision and the error ellipse.

# text
seg-b7a2c252c41c = <b>What the receiver hears</b> — the L-band spectrum under a jammer, jamming and spoofing, and tracking-loop loss of lock.

# text
seg-f354743045c4 = <b>Whether the answer can be trusted</b> — integrity monitoring (RAIM, receiver autonomous integrity monitoring, and its advanced form ARAIM) with protection levels.

# text
seg-69aa01a29a49 = <b>Alternatives to GNSS</b> — gravity and terrain map matching, and positioning, navigation and timing from low Earth orbit (LEO) satellites, from the signal design through the link budget and the navigation message to the position fix.

# text
seg-297c98214b5b = <b>Timing for networks</b> — telecom holdover checked against the international masks, and slot timing for time-indexed schedules.

# text
seg-7eba216a9bf2 = <b>Beyond Earth</b> — lunar time and reference frames, lunar service volumes and Mars relay navigation.

# text
seg-b796f49570ed = <b>Many runs at once</b> — campaigns chain kinds on one timeline, sweep a parameter or run a Monte Carlo ensemble.

# text
seg-ef7f4e025018 = { "5" }. Honesty by construction

# text
seg-65fb8703baf9 = A simulator is only useful if you can trust it. Kshana is built so that you can:

# text
# c1: provenance
seg-de8fd160db6d = <b>Every parameter is cited.</b> Each sensor figure carries a <c1/> string naming the datasheet or paper it came from. No anonymous constants.

# text
seg-09d5cd11b127 = <b>Every model is checked against a textbook relation,</b> not just against itself — e.g. the simulated clock's Allan deviation must match the published stability figure, and the inertial drift must match the standard error-growth law. A capability is called <em>validated</em> only where an independent external oracle (a real dataset, an independent implementation or published reference vectors) agrees; the rest is <em>modelled</em>, and the <a1>verification matrix</a1> says which is which.

# text
seg-ccb5b2fe93c4 = Maturity is labelled.

# text
# c1: validated
# c2: modelled
# c3: not modeled
seg-5a0ac98febed = <a1>VALIDATION.md</a1> marks each effect <c1/>, <c2/> or <c3/>, and states plainly that the optical-clock figures are <em>laboratory / space-goal</em> numbers — no strontium optical clock has flown.

# text
seg-fcc8af84343a = <b>Results are reproducible to the bit:</b> the same scenario, seed, and version always produce the identical answer on the same platform; across operating systems the figures agree to 1e-6 relative (see <a1>REPRODUCIBILITY.md</a1>).

# text
seg-f87dad1ffc98 = { "6" }. The physics, for specialists

# text
seg-8fc6db9faf83 = The relations Kshana implements and tests (full detail and tolerances in <a1>VALIDATION.md</a1>):

# text
# c1: q_wf
# c2: q_rw
# c3: σ_y(τ)
seg-ea61f869c266 = <b>Clock holdover.</b> Two-state phase/frequency model with white frequency modulation (white FM, power spectral density <c1/>), random-walk FM (<c2/>), flicker FM (a sum of log-spaced Ornstein–Uhlenbeck processes calibrated to a flat Allan floor), and deterministic aging. Validated by overlapping Allan deviation against the published <c3/> (Riley, NIST (National Institute of Standards and Technology) SP (Special Publication) { "1065" }).

# text
# c1: ½·b·T²
# c2: σ_x(T) = √(S_a·T³/3)
# c3: g·θ
seg-4f46754c399f = <b>Inertial dead-reckoning.</b> Residual accelerometer bias → <c1/>; velocity random walk → <c2/>; optional gyro bias and angular random walk produce a tilt error that couples gravity (<c3/>) into horizontal acceleration (Groves).

# text
# c1: range = c·dt
# c2: σ/√N
seg-d59595d00f0a = <b>Time transfer.</b> White timing jitter → synchronisation precision → one-way ranging (<c1/>, { "1" } ps ≈ { "0.3" } mm); the sample mean averages as <c2/>.

# text
# c1: q_wf·T + q_rw·T³/3
seg-0eb7a53fb4a6 = <b>Fusion & integrity.</b> A two-state Kalman filter whose process noise matches the truth model; coasting, its phase-error variance grows to exactly <c1/> — the analytic holdover relation — and its 1-σ bound feeds the Integrity figure of merit.

# text
seg-80daa7cb8641 = <b>Geometry.</b> Keplerian two-body propagation (optionally with secular J2) or SGP4 (Simplified General Perturbations { "4" }) from real two-line element sets, Walker constellations, and line-of-sight visibility (Earth occultation + elevation mask) derive GNSS availability from orbital geometry rather than hand-authored windows.

